T44 Calls probe — conformance transcript, refreshed by T44-AV-R on 2026-10-27
============================================================================

Probe      scripts/t44_calls_probe.py  (target: the Calls signalling contract, from outside the app)
Server     Mattermost 9.11.19, docker `mm-perf`, http://127.0.0.1:8065
Plugin     com.mattermost.calls 0.29.8, build 936dd00d3e456c843b846740aa76601d07e6a25a
Account    fixture account `perfprobe`; the probe creates the channels it joins
Fixture    `mm-calls-udp` (T44b's UDP relay, 127.0.0.1:8443 -> 8443/udp) is Up, so the media path
           reads 1/3 here — not the 0/3 of the T44 log of the same date. See
           docs/t44b-calls-media-path.md for why the relay is the fixture's documented state.

WHY THIS LOG WAS REFRESHED (T44-AV finding F1)
----------------------------------------------
The probe's two negative checks counted **any** `signal` event and never compared the payload's
`connID`. The plugin publishes `signal` to the *user*, not to the connection
(`p.publishWebSocketEvent(wsEventSignal, …, &WebSocketBroadcast{UserID: us.userID,
ReliableClusterSend: true})`, server/websocket.go:590-593 at v0.29.8), so a second live session of
the fixture account (another probe, a browser tab) delivered its answer and candidates to the
socket that was supposed to prove "a JSON-framed sdp is not answered". Measured by T44-AV:
6 PASS / 2 FAIL while T44b ran, vs 8 PASS / 0 FAIL alone.

Two things changed in the probe:

1. Every `signal` frame is attributed by `connID` and a frame whose `connID` is not this socket's
   is counted in `foreign_signal_ignored` and ignored. The socket's own `connID` comes from the
   core server's `hello` event (`data.connection_id`) — measured, not assumed: a handshake that
   supplied `?connection_id=…` was ignored by the server, `hello` answered with the id it
   assigned, and the plugin's join ack repeated exactly that id. This matters because the join
   ack is itself broadcast to the user (server/websocket.go:830-832), so with two live sessions
   *both* sockets receive *both* acks; taking the last ack as "ours" silently attributed this
   socket's frames to the other session (observed while writing this card: two concurrent probes
   reported the same connID for different joins until attribution moved to `hello`).

2. Check 8 ("the client is the offerer", which only meant "run A was not contaminated") is now a
   real no-offer session (`run C`): join, send nothing, assert **0** `signal` frames carrying this
   connection's `connID`, and observe the plugin's teardown. The teardown is attributed the same
   way: the plugin's `user_left` carries `session_id` == the `connID` of the session that left, so
   "our silent session was torn down at ~10 s" cannot be borrowed from a neighbour either.
   (A foreign `user_disconnected` is still *printed* by run C when one arrives — see the staggered
   transcript, where it lands at 0.94 s under another session's teardown — but it is not the
   signal the check reads.)

THE RUNS IN THIS FILE, IN ORDER
--------------------------------
  1. ISOLATED     one probe, nothing else signed in to the fixture account.
                  Expectation: 9 PASS / 0 FAIL, exit 0.
  2. CONCURRENT   two invocations started together, in different channels (t44-calls,
                  t44-calls-b) so neither join replaces the other. Both send MessagePack offers,
                  so each socket receives the other's answer + candidates.
                  Expectation: both 9 PASS / 0 FAIL, exit 0.
  3. STAGGERED    the sharp direction: probe A starts, probe B starts 1.5 s later, so B's answer
                  and candidates arrive **inside A's run A** — the JSON-offer window whose whole
                  point is that no answer arrives. A's run A reports `6 foreign frame(s) ignored`
                  and still passes; under the old check 5 that was the FAIL. B's no-offer session
                  ignores the same six frames (the old check 8 FAIL).
                  Expectation: both 9 PASS / 0 FAIL, exit 0.

Exit codes are printed after each transcript. The probe exits non-zero if any check fails, so
"EXIT CODE: 0" is the whole claim.

Nothing here weakens a check: the negative checks are now *narrower* (they only believe frames
that belong to the socket under test) and were made *stronger* by the same move (a frame that
belongs to this connection but should not exist now fails the check, where before any frame —
including a neighbour's — decided it).

